# External artifacts the jess oracles consume, pinned by digest.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (AFD-053 S2): every jess oracle defaulted to a path under
# .scratch/, which is gitignored. Nothing in the repo pinned what was supposed to be
# there, so 'against shipped gale-nano 0.7.0' and '10/10 digests verified' were
# asserted by FILENAME AND PROSE, not mechanically — and no one could reproduce a
# single result from a clean checkout. These digests make the claim checkable.
#
# Verified 2026-09-02: the five falcon components already in .scratch/v1341 are
# BYTE-IDENTICAL to relay's published falcon-v1.134.1 assets, so this pins what was
# actually used to produce AFD-046/-051/-052, not a lookalike.
#
# format:  <dest-path-under-.scratch>  <sha256>  <locator>
#
# The locator is what tools/deps/fetch.sh uses to RECONSTRUCT the byte from a clean
# checkout. Until 2026-09-04 these said only which release the artifact came from, which
# is not enough to fetch it — the upstream NAMES differ from the local ones. Two facts
# cost real time to establish and are recorded here so nobody re-derives them:
#
#   * The falcon stages are NOT standalone release assets. They live inside
#     falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz and are named falcon-<stage>-v1.134.wasm upstream.
#     The mapping below was established BY DIGEST, not by guessing names.
#   * gale-nano is in NO gale release (checked v0.7.0/v0.7.1/v0.8.0 — the release carries
#     only the msgq/mutex/sem primitives). It is an OCI artifact at
#     ghcr.io/pulseengine/gale-nano:0.7.0, where its name is fused-gustos.component.wasm.
#
# grammar:
#   release:<owner>/<repo>@<tag>!<asset>[!<member-inside-tarball>]
#   oci:<ref>!<file-in-artifact>
#
# PLATFORM-SPECIFIC PINS: synthpin/synth is a compiled BINARY, so its digest differs per host.
# There is therefore ONE LINE PER PLATFORM, each tagged `platform=`, and both check.sh and
# fetch.sh act only on the line matching the running host and SKIP the others visibly. A
# single-platform pin was what blocked the on-target oracles from becoming a CI gate
# (AFD-064 D4): a linux runner could never satisfy a Mach-O digest.
#
# Each linux digest below was taken from the binary inside the platform tarball AFTER
# verifying that tarball against the release's own signed SHA256SUMS.txt — so the chain is
# release signature -> tarball -> extracted binary, not a digest someone typed in.

[falcon-v1.134.1]
v1341/rate.wasm              43982be498c0e4a2b03ce6a56a06496cb46dcdc98fdf8bf8cbd610a1d891ae63  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.134.1!falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz!falcon-rate-v1.134.wasm
v1341/mixer.wasm             076b8a816493e1997191f459c624c0a5b7b80fdcc0c97a4e039d7d24aaa789b3  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.134.1!falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz!falcon-mixer-v1.134.wasm
v1341/attitude.wasm          d85c325f7564145b99454a60b207d9409f6b600668cdcbde0157f329d68910d1  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.134.1!falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz!falcon-attitude-v1.134.wasm
v1341/position.wasm          32a45a53aa9fa3a4193aedf4f82cbfaa16810130df9320d0e048958d258eba5b  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.134.1!falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz!falcon-position-v1.134.wasm
v1341/iekf.wasm              600f9047dba73f278d80b8a0a904bb2066eea33073902fee84aea3d35bb09f0b  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.134.1!falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz!falcon-iekf-v1.134.wasm

[gale-nano]
galenano7/gale-nano-0.7.0.wasm 546531952a5cf0edb05ea804bb77dea9ec2786530cef8310bd718559eeede86f  oci:ghcr.io/pulseengine/gale-nano:0.7.0!fused-gustos.component.wasm

[synth]
# NOT varve-pinned: the varve layer carries synth 0.58.0 while the campaign runs 0.64.0.
#
# BUMPED 0.60.0 -> 0.64.0 for synth#1189: on the ARM DIRECT SELECTOR — which is "every
# --relocatable compile", i.e. every lowering jess does — an if/else join register could
# ALIAS a live register-homed local, so `local.get` after the join read the join's value.
# Exit 0, no decline, WRONG ANSWER. Measured under 0.60.0:
#     c: mov  r0, r2      <- join writes r0, which IS local 0's home
#     e: adds r3, r0, r0  <- if-result + local.get 0, both r0
# 0.64.0 copies the home into a temp first and reads r0 intact.
#
# THE BUMP IS PROVABLY BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL TODAY. Every artifact jess lowers is BYTE-IDENTICAL
# across 0.60.0 -> 0.64.0: the fused cascade (m7dp, m4f), gale-nano 0.7.0 (m3), the
# f100-init and gust:hal probes (m3), and the passthrough image that runs on real F100
# silicon (which also still matches its committed .elf, md5 13495328...). And the
# differential is NOT vacuous: a module carrying the #1189 shape DOES differ (504 -> 510 B).
# So jess was never exposed, and the pin now excludes a silent wrong-answer class.
# That divergence is real and is why this is pinned by digest here (AFD-053 S5).
synthpin/synth               fa96245bf55e562d70b058e72316dcef6e3c9413d83cdff1019b5a41e1134d02  release:pulseengine/synth@v0.64.0!synth-v0.64.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!synth      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
synthpin/synth               5f7042ac07d30b40d58161ec0e7ed5fec23020bfc1589051a1a752c94bc6156c  release:pulseengine/synth@v0.64.0!synth-v0.64.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!synth  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu

[toolchain]
# The lowering toolchain, pinned per platform so a CI runner can reconstruct the SAME BYTES
# the varve layer dispatches locally. VERIFIED 2026-09-04: the binaries inside these release
# tarballs are byte-identical to what `varve which meld` / `varve which loom` resolve to, so
# a result measured in CI is comparable to one measured on a developer machine rather than
# merely similar.
# BUMPED 0.52.0 -> 0.55.1. jess REPORTED two of the defects its own pin still contained:
#   0.53.0  the shared-memory/rebase path could emit INVALID wasm at exit 0 (jess: meld#390)
#   0.54.0  it could emit a module that VALIDATES and returns the WRONG NUMBER (same thread)
#   0.55.0  --memory shared now validates its own output by DEFAULT — the change jess argued for
#   0.55.1  the attestation's components_fused read the FLATTENED list (jess: meld#401)
# jess fuses with --memory shared --pack-rebase, so all four are on jess's exact path.
#
# BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL, MEASURED END TO END. Fused CODE+DATA byte-identical 0.52.0 -> 0.55.1
# (7,949 identical lines with both metadata sections stripped), the full chain builds
# (89,816 B linked, 0 undefined, 5/5 key symbols, fusion still byte-reproducible), and the
# lowered ARM objects still match the AFD-106 baseline exactly: m7dp 5362d831..., m4f
# 90e8fa9b.... So the defect classes never fired on jess's inputs — and the pin no longer
# carries them.
#
# What the bump DOES change is the attested count: 5 input files were attested as 10
# components under 0.52.0 and are attested as 5 under 0.55.1, with the console line agreeing.
# The attestation is the half that travels with the artifact and that an auditor reads.
meld/meld                    8ac67e9324db09d1028fef84f086a16b3a988898e8dc7a82f4e0aad8a2dc3563  release:pulseengine/meld@v0.55.1!meld-v0.55.1-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!meld      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
meld/meld                    d8be7ee18fc0b06dc85f65133eb7938b41480f65dfbb3ed6c05efbaa2d4b3d94  release:pulseengine/meld@v0.55.1!meld-v0.55.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!meld  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
loom/loom                    9583f530baa0d6f0a0c140b5247ac618d9d56bcbf8d42e0c8a1ba5a2a20af6f6  release:pulseengine/loom@v1.4.0!loom-v1.4.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!loom      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
loom/loom                    5d22f8ffe1aedfb33476698978a22d93372b9214198dac33cbc6fb5df15c5380  release:pulseengine/loom@v1.4.0!loom-v1.4.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!loom  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu

[h1-bringup]
# NXP's i.MX RT1176 FLASHLOADER — required for H1 on real silicon.
#
# WHY IT IS NEEDED: spsdk's own device database says the RT117x ROM speaks SDP
# (usb 0x1FC9:0x013D) and that blhost/mboot (usb 0x15A2:0x0073) is only reachable AFTER a
# flashloader is running. blhost's write-memory / call / read-memory — which is exactly the
# H1 shape — therefore need this binary loaded into OCRAM first. NXP community posts claim
# RT11xx can skip it; the database disagrees, and the first read-only nxpdevscan in ISP
# settles which is true before anything is written.
#
# PROVENANCE, because this executes on the flight controller: it ships inside a TOOL repo,
# not as a release asset. Fetched from JayHeng/NXP-MCUBootUtility and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL
# to the copy in NXP's OWN org (nxp-mcuxpresso/mcu-boot-utility) — two independent sources,
# one of them the vendor. Structurally checked too: valid i.MX IVT (tag 0xD1, len 32, ver
# 0x40), entry 0x20262561 and self 0x2024FE00, both in OCRAM1 where a flashloader belongs.
# It is UNSIGNED (csf=0), which is normal for a flashloader but worth stating.
h1/ivt_flashloader.bin       ba6a8b06161aef7914436909185b6196b278f8e4cd16deeaab9945c1464d5f41  ghfile:nxp-mcuxpresso/mcu-boot-utility@master!src/targets/MIMXRT1176/ivt_flashloader.bin
# PX4's OWN command-line NSH console over MAVLink SERIAL_CONTROL. This is how `reboot -i`
# is issued without QGroundControl. Using the vendor's tool is the same category as using
# blhost or nxpdevscan — it is NOT jess building a second MAVLink stack, which is on the
# do-not-repeat list. relay owns MAVLink in this org and ships a COMMAND_LONG codec, but no
# SERIAL_CONTROL; `reboot -i` needs the console pipe, not the command interface.
#
# NOT READ-ONLY: mavlink_shell.py sends a heartbeat on connect (line 48,
# `self.mav.mav.heartbeat_send(...)`) before any command is typed. Merely attaching it is a
# WRITE to the vehicle and requires the operator's go-ahead, unlike an O_RDONLY tty capture.
#
# Pinned to the immutable commit that last touched the file, NOT @main, so the digest stays
# valid when PX4 moves.
h1/mavlink_shell.py          28cc4554d9eedf4670d1e1e200a858af4caeeab42034ed51214c3f1ab951844e  ghfile:PX4/PX4-Autopilot@de166e3a09e396a2fa1c28aa9fc014f109b25a10!Tools/mavlink_shell.py

